<!-- formulate jump-diffusions in terms of characteristics -->
As in [Definition III.2.18](/citations#jacod2003), a $(\Prb, \scrF)$ jump-diffusion $\X$ on state space $(\stateSpace, \scrB(\stateSpace))$ is a $(\Prb, \scrF)$ semimartingale in which the $\trunc$-characteristics $(B^\trunc, A, \predproj{\jumpMeas^\X})$ have the following decompositions.
$$
  @tag(eq-jump-diffusion-characteristics)
  B^\trunc_t = \int_0^t \affDrift^\trunc(\X_s) \rmd s, \quad
  A_t = \int_0^t \affDiff(\X_s) \rmd s, \quad
  \predproj{\jumpMeas^\X}(\rmd s,\rmd\markVar) = \affJump(\X_s, \rmd\markVar) \rmd s,
$$
where the functions have the following properties.

- $\affDrift^\trunc: \stateSpace \rightarrow \vecSpace$ is Borel measurable, $\affDrift^\trunc \in \scrB(\stateSpace)/\scrB(\vecSpace)$.
- $\affDiff: \stateSpace \rightarrow \bbL(\vecSpace)$ is Borel measurable, $\affDiff \in \scrB(\stateSpace)/\scrB(\bbL(\vecSpace))$, and $\affDiff(\stateVar)$ is self-adjoint and non-negative for each $\stateVar \in \stateSpace$.
- $\affJump: \stateSpace \times \scrB(\vecSpace) \rightarrow [0,\infty]$ is a transition kernel from $\stateSpace$ to $\vecSpace$, and it satisfies the following properties for each $\stateVar \in \stateSpace$.
  $$
    @tag(eq-affJump-conditions)
    \affJump\big(\stateVar, \{0\}\big) = 0, \quad \int_\vecSpace \big( 1 \wedge |\markVar|^2\big) \affJump(\stateVar, \rmd\markVar) < \infty
  $$

In other words, our jump-diffusion $\X$ has the following canonical semimartingale representation (see [Theorem II.2.34](/citations#jacod2003) for definition).
$$
  @tag(eq-jump-diffusion-canonical)
  \begin{gathered}
    \X = \X_0 + \affDrift^\trunc(\X) \shortInt \leb + \X^\rmc +  \trunc \ast \compensate{\jumpMeas^\X} + (\id_\vecSpace - \trunc) \ast \jumpMeas^\X \\
    \prj{\X^{\rmc,i}}{\X^{\rmc,j}} = \affDiff_{ij}(\X) \shortInt \leb \\
    \predproj{\jumpMeas^\X}(\rmd s, \rmd\markVar) = \affJump(\X_s, \rmd\markVar) \rmd s
  \end{gathered}
$$

<!-- a quick remark on differences and terminology. -->
<Statement {...jumpDiffusionDefinitionRemarks} />

<!-- a quick example to demonstrate Brownian motion and Poisson. -->
<Statement {...jumpDiffusionExample} />

The following Lemma will be repeatedly used as a shortcut of Itô's formula and various identities that always apply with jump-diffusions.

<!-- restate Ito in terms of jump-diffusions -->
<Statement {...jumpDiffusionIto} />

<!-- express the notion of the generator of X -->
In the above lemma, the final term in the semimartingale decomposition of $f(\X)$ is typically not able to be compensated into a local martingale.
If we did have local integrability of the following quantity,
$$
  \Big| f(\X_-+\id_\vecSpace) - f(\X_-) + \Der f(\X_-) \cdot \trunc \Big| \ast \predproj{\jumpMeas^\X},
$$
then by [Proposition II.1.28](/citations#jacod2003) we could rewrite $f(\X)$ into a canonical special semimartingale decomposition.
$$
  @tag(eq-jump-diffusion-generator)
  \begin{aligned}
    f(\X_t)
    &= f(\X_0) + \generator f(\X) \shortInt \leb_t + \Der f(\X_-) \shortInt \X^\rmc + \big( f(\X_- + \id_\vecSpace) - f(\X_-) \big) \ast \compensate{\jumpMeas^\X}_t \\
    \generator f(\stateVar)
    &\defeq \Der f(\stateVar) \cdot \affDrift^\trunc(\stateVar) + \frac12 \tr\Big( \Hess f(\stateVar) \circ \affDiff(\stateVar) \Big) \\
      &\hspace{25mm}+ \int_\vecSpace \Big( f(\stateVar + \markVar) - f(\stateVar) - \Der f(\stateVar) \cdot \trunc(\markVar) \Big) \affJump(\stateVar, \rmd\markVar)
  \end{aligned}
$$
So long as $f$ is bounded, we can guarantee this special semimartingale property.

<!-- bounded -->
<Statement {...jumpDiffusionItoBounded} />

This operator $\generator$ in [](tag:eq-jump-diffusion-generator) gives a nice closed from for suitable $f(\X)$, and so we reserve it the term of _generator_ associated with $\X$.
Note that we do not mark dependence on $\trunc$, as any other truncation function $\hat\trunc$ will produce the same operator; see [%full (c)](/statements/jump-diffusions) and note that the displacement from $\affDrift^\trunc$ and $\affDrift^{\hat\trunc}$ would be the same as that in the integral term.
One particular setting in which this result is useful is establishing a Lévy-Khintchine formula for jump-diffusions.

<!-- Levy-Khintchine -->
<Statement {...jumpDiffusionLk} />

<!-- manifest equivalence -->
It turns out that each of the preceding results is sufficient in characterizing a semimartingale $\X$ as a jump-diffusion.

<Statement {...jumpDiffusionCharacterization} />
<Statement {...jumpDiffusionCharacterizationRemark} />
